&lt;div class=&quot;content-intro&quot;&gt;&lt;h2&gt;&lt;strong&gt;About Anthropic&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Anthropic’s mission is to create reliable, interpretable, and steerable AI systems. We want AI to be safe and beneficial for our users and for society as a whole. Our team is a quickly growing group of committed researchers, engineers, policy experts, and business leaders working together to build beneficial AI systems.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;h2&gt;About the role&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Anthropic is building custom silicon for Claude. The hardware our models run on is one of the most direct levers we have on capability, cost, and reliability — and we&#39;ve decided to take design authority over it ourselves.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We&#39;re looking for a Technical Program Manager to own end-to-end execution of our silicon, from architecture definition through tapeout, first-silicon bring-up, and production ramp. You&#39;ll be working alongside a small group of domain experts across front-end design, verification, physical design, DFT, analog/mixed-signal, foundry, and packaging. There is no process to inherit — you&#39;ll build the execution machinery this program runs on.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This is a partner-heavy program by design. You&#39;ll drive a portfolio of external engagements — ASIC design services, IP vendors, foundry, and OSAT. You should have shipped chips before, know what a realistic silicon schedule looks like and its failure modes, and be comfortable making consequential program calls without a large organization behind you. And because this is Anthropic, you&#39;ll have Claude as a collaborator — we expect the TPMs on this team to push hard on what AI-assisted program management can look like.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Key responsibilities&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Own the integrated silicon program plan — architecture closure, RTL freeze, DV milestones, IP delivery, PD closure, DFT, tapeout, and post-silicon bring-up.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Drive external partner execution across ASIC design partners, IP vendors, foundry, and packaging/test.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Run technology and IP selection as a program: track candidate vendors and options, drive evaluations to a decision, and land the build / buy / license calls with engineering leadership.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Build the execution mechanisms a silicon team needs — milestone definitions, risk and dependency tracking, design reviews, release management, exec reporting — and keep them lightweight enough that engineers actually use them.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Manage the interface between silicon and its consumers: hardware systems for package/board/bring-up, and inference/kernels/compiler teams for hardware-software co-design, so trade-offs land as coordinated decisions rather than late surprises.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Own the post-silicon program — bring-up planning, ATE/test handoff, sample distribution, validation, and the path to production readiness.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Develop and share AI-assisted approaches to program management — schedule and dependency modeling, spec and milestone consistency checking, partner-deliverable tracking, status synthesis.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Minimum qualifications&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;8+ years in silicon/SoC program management, chip lead, or an equivalent role that owned a chip schedule end to end.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Direct ownership of at least one program that taped out and reached production, with milestones you can speak to in detail — including where it slipped and what you did about it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Working technical fluency across the silicon lifecycle — front-end, DV, PD, DFT, foundry, packaging.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Experience running external silicon partners — ASIC houses, IP vendors, foundry, or OSAT — including SOW scoping, delivery tracking, and escalation.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Track record of building program mechanisms from scratch on an ambiguous, fast-moving effort.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Strong written communication — plans, reviews, and exec updates that many teams depend on.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Comfort operating with high autonomy and limited process on a highly dynamic program.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Preferred qualifications&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Experience on ML accelerators, high-performance compute, networking, or other large, high-bandwidth SoCs at advanced process nodes.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Experience on a first-generation or early silicon program, including standing up flows, methodology, or vendor relationships from nothing.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Experience with both partner-executed and fully in-house programs, and a view on the trade-offs between them.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Prior hands-on engineering background in one or more silicon domains (design, DV, PD, DFT, or SI/PI).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Familiarity with the chip-package-system interface and co-design with a hardware systems team.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Experience with hardware-software co-design programs — working directly with compiler, kernel, or runtime teams.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Practical experience using AI tools in your own workflow, and clear ideas about where they help and where they don&#39;t.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;BS/MS in EE, CE, CS, or a related field, or equivalent experience.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;&lt;div class=&quot;content-pay-transparency&quot;&gt;&lt;div class=&quot;pay-input&quot;&gt;&lt;div class=&quot;description&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;The annual compensation range for this role is listed below.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
